{
  "calldataIntrinsicGasAcrossUpgrades": {
    "_info": {
      "comment": "What a transaction's payload costs before its code begins to run. This is the first fixture here to measure INTRINSIC gas rather than the cost of an instruction: the charge is levied on the transaction's data by the state transition itself, so no opcode can be timed to find it. GAS as the very first instruction reports the limit minus everything already deducted, which makes the intrinsic charge readable from inside a contract that does nothing else. Both payloads are a hundred bytes; index 0 is all zeroes and index 1 is all non-zero, so the difference between them is purely the per-byte pricing and the fixed 21,000 cancels. Index 0 is the control and it does not move at any upgrade, because the price of a zero byte has never changed -- that flatness is what makes the other column's single step attributable. Through Agharta a hundred non-zero bytes cost 6,400 more than a hundred zero bytes, which is 64 each: 68 against 4. From Phoenix it is 1,200, which is 12 each: 16 against 4. Slot 1 is a liveness marker.",
      "oracle": "core-geth production, evm t8n at each upgrade's rules, chain id 61",
      "oracle-version": "v1.12.21-unstable",
      "generated": "2026-08-23",
      "signing": "txbytes carries the signed transaction; the signing key is a filling input, not a fixture field, and the harness reads `sender`.",
      "eip": "EIP-2028 at Phoenix, reducing the non-zero calldata byte from 68 gas to 16",
      "control": "Data index 0 is a hundred zero bytes and must report the same remaining gas at every upgrade. If it moves, something other than the non-zero byte price changed and the reading for index 1 is not attributable.",
      "why-not-an-opcode": "CALLDATASIZE and CALLDATACOPY price reading the payload, not carrying it. The charge this asserts is paid before execution starts and is invisible to every instruction except the first GAS.",
      "unfilled-no-fork-name": "No expectation for ETC_Thanos: the generator's fork table has no name for them, so their rules cannot be selected. A tooling gap, not a claim that the case does not apply.",
      "unfilled-not-a-state-rule": "No expectation for ETC_DAOFork, ETC_DAOWars, ETC_FrontierThawing, ETC_MESSOff, ETC_MESSOn: these change no state-transition rule, so a state fixture has nothing to assert about them. Covering them needs block-level or chain-selection tests."
    },
